        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "Harvard, MIT, and University of Washington researchers found that AI-generated explanations for recommendations can suppress independent human judgment rather than enhance it. In an innovation evaluation study, evaluators given AI explanations rejected more promising ideas than those given black-box recommendations without narratives. The findings suggest organizations should design AI systems that preserve, rather than replace, human discretion.",
        "execSummary":  "This research delivers a direct warning for enterprise AI deployments: adding AI explanations to recommendations may actually degrade decision quality by anchoring human judgment to the AI\u0027s framing. For customer support leaders overseeing AI-assisted case handling or escalation routing, this finding argues against surfacing AI rationale by default. The design implication is significant — human-AI collaboration interfaces should be tested, not assumed to be beneficial. Leaders deploying explainable AI should validate whether explanations improve or impair their teams\u0027 outcomes.",
